# How to cite dnoise. Read by GitHub (the "Cite this repository" button) and by
# Zenodo, which uses it to populate the metadata of the archived release rather
# than guessing from the repository name.
#
# Two things to update at release time:
#   - `version` and `date-released` must match the tag being archived.
#   - once Zenodo has minted a DOI, add it under `identifiers` so the record is
#     self-describing; and once the paper is published, fill in `preferred-citation`
#     with its DOI so citing the software points readers at the paper.
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it using the metadata below."
title: dnoise
abstract: >-
  Fast, in-place denoising of Bruker timsTOF .d files. Real ions form streaks
  along the ion-mobility axis whereas chemical and electronic noise is short,
  isolated, or scattered; dnoise exploits that structural prior to remove noise
  from MS1 (and optionally MS/MS) frames, writing cleaned .d folders that stay
  drop-in compatible with existing tools. No per-dataset calibration is required.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Patrick
    family-names: Garrett
    email: pgarrett@scripps.edu
    affiliation: >-
      Department of Chemical Physiology, The Scripps Research Institute,
      La Jolla, CA, USA
  - given-names: Jolene K.
    family-names: Diedrich
    affiliation: >-
      Multi-Omics Core, The Scripps Research Institute, La Jolla, CA, USA
  - given-names: John R.
    name-suffix: III
    family-names: Yates
    affiliation: >-
      Department of Chemical Physiology, The Scripps Research Institute,
      La Jolla, CA, USA
repository-code: "https://github.com/pgarrett-scripps/dnoise"
url: "https://github.com/pgarrett-scripps/dnoise"
license: MIT
version: 0.1.0
date-released: "2026-08-01"
keywords:
  - mass spectrometry
  - proteomics
  - timsTOF
  - ion mobility
  - denoising
  - PASEF
  - Bruker
